# Come Join Us, Tamils!

*[This article was written by Comrade C. N. Annadurai, M.A., about the tour of Tamil Nadu undertaken by Comrades Eelathadigal, A. Ponnambalanar, editor of *Viduthalai*, and C. N. Annadurai.]*

You know Comrade Ponnambalanar well. A young bull who is the embodiment of labour, the dwelling place of enthusiasm, a wellspring of feeling, and Periyar's right hand. Today he is editor of *Viduthalai*, the drum of the Tamils. With affection he ordered both me, who had been released from prison but remained afflicted by ill health, and Sivanantha Adigal of Eelam to travel somehow to various places in Tamil Nadu and conduct propaganda for at least a month.

He himself prepared all the arrangements and the programme schedule, most skilfully. You see, he is not merely a speaker; he has demonstrated his skill in organising constructive work for our movement.

The scenes we witnessed in every town we visited truly gave us profound inspiration. We can never forget the young people who gathered in each town to welcome him, the traders who greeted us, the workers who described their suffering, the public who assembled in thousands to enjoy the speeches, and the people who openly displayed their resentment towards the Congress Party.

Weariness and exhaustion had sometimes made us wonder whether the public was learning our principles, whether our labour was yielding even some benefit, whether the light of Periyar's propaganda had reached them, and whether the Tamil party would gain influence. This tour completely removed those doubts. It also led us to conclude that victory would be possible if we intensified our propaganda a little further and began undertaking constructive work.

The changes in Karur, Tiruppur, and Madurai were particularly astonishing. Karur had witnessed the working ability of Eelathadigal, so it was natural that it should have undergone a great change. Working alone, running everywhere and roaming tirelessly like a spirit, Karur Adigal sowed the seeds of the Tamil movement there by writing until his hand ached. Today many young people of influence, purity of heart, and feeling are present there to continue the work he began. Some of them are former Congressmen. Condemning the Congress Party for seizing office and keeping the people impoverished, the young people work with great intensity. Tamil flags fly higher and higher. Tamil associations and South Indian Welfare Rights associations are being established. What feeling and enthusiasm we saw at the meeting where we spoke that day!

And not only Karur. What can be said of Tiruppur? It was a magnificent gathering. The most important and heartening scene we saw there was the Muslim League volunteer corps, dressed in green, bearing the League flag, sounding its drum, and marching in such a beautiful formation. Similar volunteer corps must be created everywhere. In every place we visited, people said they needed such a corps. We said that these "sons of Bharata" were not wanted, and we wish to tell all of you: organise volunteer corps in every locality.

In Palani, the Muslim League, the Tamil association, and the traders all possess excellent feeling. There are good party workers. If they set their minds to it, they can gather immense strength for our movement. The speech Comrade Ponnambalam delivered at the Palani meeting was unlike anything he spoke elsewhere. It was an excellent address. Our plight became difficult when we had to begin speaking after him that day! The true purpose of our movement was explained well to Palani.

I had heard some say that Coimbatore contained various Congress propagandists and leaders and was a Congress fortress. But when I think of what we saw on the day we visited, I can say that there is no place where our strength can penetrate and become established as firmly as in Coimbatore. We found leaders there capable of doing such work.

At the same time as our meeting, a Congress meeting was held elsewhere in the city. Even so, our meeting overflowed with feeling. Some labour leaders reportedly spoke at the Congress meeting. Unexpectedly, at our meeting there was strong condemnation of the conduct of sham labour leaders in Madras who live by invoking the name of a festival estimated to bring in as much as twenty crore rupees.

Even while staying in Coimbatore, our minds had travelled to Thoothukudi. We heard that a grand conference was to be conducted there. Thoothukudi possesses hundreds of Tamil heroes with seething minds, overflowing party enthusiasm, and the necessary means. Who can forget the tireless service of the comrades of Shanmugapuram, where the flame of the Self-Respect Movement shines? Was it not another distinction that the Paduvai and Ilanji families came forward to help the volunteers participate splendidly in that region?

We leapt with excitement and went to Thoothukudi. The moment the train stopped, we beheld a sight that seized our eyes. Young bulls, no older than thirty and no younger than fifteen, with strongly built bodies, full cheeks, keen gazes, and red uniforms: what a sight that volunteer corps made, standing in formation at the railway station to welcome our people!

The procession held there that day was also magnificent, beautiful enough to fill even religious-minded hearts with delight. We tried to pass through the whole city and finally reached the conference pavilion. Neither the conference nor the evening public meeting had ever been matched in that region. If as many as fifteen hundred women alone attended the public meeting, need the distinction of that conference be described further?

(*Kudi Arasu*, 14-5-1939)
